course: money
lesson: 23
title: When money is tight: "we can't afford it"
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: "We can't afford it" means there is not enough money left after the important things, like rent, food and bills; it is not a punishment, and it has nothing to do with how much a child is loved.
explain: Picture Leo asking for a £200 bike when his family has £50 spare this month: they are £150 short, so the answer is "not now", even though Mum would love to say yes.
explain: Lots of good, hard-working families go through tight months: a job ends, a bill jumps, someone gets ill; food banks, free school meals and second-hand shops exist exactly for times like that, and there is no shame in using them.
explain: Children are never to blame for money worries; a hug, a bit of help and not asking for extras can mean a lot, and a trusted adult is there to talk to if worry gets heavy.
explain: Making fun of someone's cheap trainers or free lunch is unkind and wrong; what matters is who a person is, not what they wear.
fact: To afford something means to have enough money for it after the important things are paid.
fact: A £200 bike with only £50 spare leaves the family £200 − £50 = £150 short.
fact: Money worries are stress about paying for things; they are grown-ups' job to sort, and children are never to blame.
fact: Laughing at someone for having less money is unkind; trainers at £20 keep feet dry just like trainers at £80.
fact: A food bank is a place that gives free food to people who need it; many families use one when money runs short, and it is nothing to be ashamed of.
fact: Free school meals are lunches paid for by the government for families who need them; at about £2.50 a day, they save a family £12.50 every five-day school week.
fact: "Skint" is everyday British slang for having very little money.
fact: Free fun like the park, the library and a picnic costs £0, while the cinema for 4 at £8 each costs £32.
fact: A trade-off is giving up one thing to pay for another, like skipping a £15 takeaway for 2 weeks to save £30 for a school trip.
fact: Second-hand means used by someone before; shoes for £10 instead of £40 cost a quarter of the price, because £40 ÷ 4 = £10.
fact: A charity shop sells donated things and gives the money to a good cause.
fact: Communities help each other: a family that receives a £40 hamper one year may give one the next.
fact: When grown-ups are stressed about money they may be snappy, but they still love their children; talking to a trusted adult helps a worried child.
exercise: Mum says, "We can't afford it right now." What does that mean? | There isn't enough money left after the important things | Mum doesn't want Leo to be happy | Leo is being punished for something naughty he did last week | The bike shop refuses to sell to children
exercise: The bike costs £200 and the family has £50 spare. How much are they short? | £150 | £250 | £50 | £200
exercise: Hannah sees Mum upset over a letter about money. What helps most? | A hug and a bit of help around the house | Asking for a new toy to cheer her up | Trying to pay the bill with her pocket money | Pretending she saw nothing and asking for sweets
exercise: Tom's trainers cost £20 and his mate's cost £80. How much more did the mate's cost? | £60 | £100 | £40 | £4
exercise: Tom's mate laughs at his cheap trainers. What is true? | Laughing at someone for having less money is unkind and wrong | Cheaper trainers mean Tom is a worse person | Only people with £80 trainers can be proper friends with each other | The teacher should buy Tom new trainers
exercise: What is a food bank? | A place that gives free food to people in need | A bank that keeps people's money hidden inside tins of food | A shop that sells food at double the normal price | A safe place where restaurants keep their spare food
exercise: Amira worries people will find out her family used a food bank. What is the truth? | Lots of good families need help sometimes; there is no shame in it | Only lazy families ever use food banks | Using a food bank is against the law | Food banks are only for people with no house
exercise: A school lunch costs about £2.50. How much do free school meals save a family over a 5-day school week? | £12.50 | £10.50 | £7.50 | £125
exercise: What are free school meals for? | Making sure all children eat well when families need help | Rewarding children who get top marks | Feeding only the teachers at lunchtime | Selling cheap lunches every day so the school can make money
exercise: The cinema costs £8 each for 4 people. How much is saved by going to the park instead? | £32 | £12 | £8 | £24
exercise: Ruby asks, "Is this cos we're skint?" What does "skint" mean? | Having very little money | Being too tired to go out | Having a sore throat | Being late for something
exercise: A takeaway costs £15 a week. How much is saved by skipping it for 2 weeks? | £30 | £15 | £17 | £45
exercise: Ellie's mum skips the takeaway to pay for the trip. What is that called? | A trade-off: giving up one thing to pay for another | A loan: borrowing money from the bank and paying it back | A refund: getting your money back from a shop after buying | A profit: money left over after selling something for more
exercise: New shoes cost £40 and second-hand ones £10. What fraction of the price is that? | A quarter | A half | A third | A tenth
exercise: Callum hears his parents arguing about money and thinks it's his fault. What is true? | It's not his fault; grown-ups get stressed but still love him | It is his fault for asking for too many presents last birthday | He should leave home so they spend less | Children cause money problems by eating too much
exercise: Theo's family gets a Christmas hamper worth about £40 from the school. What is the best way to see it? | People are kind, and next time the family may give one | It means the family must have done something wrong to need it | It is a prize for the worst-off family in school | Taking help means you can never give help later
test: A kid at school goes, "Free school dinners? Lol, you're poor." What is the best answer? | Free school meals help loads of kids, and mocking that is out of order | He's right, so the kid should hide his lunch | Free meals are worse food, so he has a point | Only children who are naughty get free meals
test: Your mate, proper Mancunian, says, "Me mam got me trainers from the charity shop, they're sound, our kid." ("Sound" means good; "our kid" means brother or mate.) What's true? | Second-hand trainers can be as good as new for a lot less money | Charity shops only sell broken things that nobody else wants to buy | Second-hand trainers cost more than new ones | It's against the rules to wear second-hand shoes at school
test: What would happen if Leo's mum bought the £200 bike with only £50 spare? | She'd be £150 short for other bills like food and energy | Nothing, because bikes are free to keep after the first month | The family would just have £250 spare at the end of the month | The bike shop would pay the energy bill for them as a thank-you
test: Gran in Newcastle says to Callum, "Divvn't fret, pet, it's not your fault, man." ("Divvn't" means don't; "fret" means worry.) What is she telling him? | Not to worry: grown-ups' money stress isn't his fault | To fetch his pet hamster so that he feels a bit better | That he must pay for the family's shopping from now on | That he did something wrong and must say sorry to them
test: Leo storms off: "Ugh, you never buy me anything, you don't even care!" What's actually going on? | His parents care but there isn't enough money left this month | His parents have decided to punish him, though he did nothing wrong | His parents have plenty of money in the bank but are hiding it | His parents think bikes are far too dangerous for all children
test: A cartoon shows a sad family "fixing" money problems by winning the lottery. What does it get wrong? | Most families get through tight times with plans, help and small savings, not luck | Every family with money worries wins the lottery in the end | Lotteries give money to anyone who is sad | Real families always fix money problems overnight
test: Dad says, "Free fun this month — park, library, picnic." Your cousin goes, "That's so mid, no cap." ("Mid" means boring; "no cap" means honestly.) What's the best reply? | Free fun can be just as good, and it saves money when things are tight | He's right, fun only counts if you pay for it | Libraries charge more than cinemas anyway | Parks are closed to families who are skint
test: Ellie's mum says, "I'll find the £30, but no takeaway for a bit." The takeaway is £15 a week. How many weeks without it pay for the trip? | 2 weeks | 3 weeks | 15 weeks | 30 weeks
test: Your mate whispers, "Amira's lot used the food bank, that's well embarrassing." What's the kindest true answer? | Loads of families need a hand sometimes; it's nothing to be ashamed of | He's right, she should never come to school again | Food banks are only for people who don't work | It's a secret club, so it doesn't count
test: Sami's mum got second-hand shoes for £10 instead of £40 — no wait, they were £12 — instead of £40. How much did she save? | £28 | £30 | £52 | £12
test: Hannah wants to pay the £300 the family is short with her £5 pocket money. What is wise? | It's grown-ups' job to sort; she can help with kindness and not asking for extras | She should take money from Mum's purse to help | She should sell the family telly herself | Her £5 will fix the problem completely
exam: It is given that a bicycle is priced at £200, whereas the sum remaining to a household after essential expenditure is £50. Determine the shortfall. | £150 | £250 | £200 | £50
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a food bank? | An organisation that distributes food without charge to persons in need | An institution that accepts deposits of food in exchange for interest | A retail outlet that sells provisions at an elevated price | A facility in which restaurants store surplus produce for sale
exam: Ascertain the aggregate saving to a household in respect of free school meals, given that each meal would otherwise cost £2.50 and that the school week comprises five days. | £12.50 | £10.50 | £125 | £7.50
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the responsibility of children for the financial difficulties of their households. | Children bear no responsibility for such difficulties | Children bear principal responsibility for such difficulties | Children are obliged by law to remedy such difficulties | Children bear responsibility only where they have requested gifts
